WEBVTT

NOTE Module 09 transcript — auto-aligned to narration.wav (125.8s). en.

1
00:00:00.154 --> 00:00:02.447
Welcome to Module Nine — time-series forecasting.

2
00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:07.731
Sequential data has rules of its own, and breaking them quietly ruins models.

3
00:00:08.062 --> 00:00:12.022
We'll cover preprocessing, windowing, model choices, multi-step forecasting, and anomaly detection.

4
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.044
Always plot the series first — look for trend, seasonality, and sudden level shifts.

5
00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:27.972
Differencing removes a trend; seasonal decomposition splits the signal into trend, seasonal, and residual parts; rolling mean and standard deviation let you eyeball stationarity.

6
00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:34.549
And never scale using statistics from the full series — that leaks the future into your training set.

7
00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:39.072
To learn from it, you reframe the series as supervised samples.

8
00:00:39.401 --> 00:00:50.318
Slide a window of length L across the data: the past L steps are your inputs, the next step or next few are the target.

9
00:00:50.672 --> 00:00:58.365
Choose expanding or rolling windows, split by time rather than shuffling, and size the window to a meaningful look-back.

10
00:00:58.721 --> 00:01:00.171
Now pick a model.

11
00:01:00.509 --> 00:01:05.095
A one-D CNN is fast and catches local patterns.

12
00:01:05.428 --> 00:01:11.925
An LSTM remembers longer context but costs you speed, and a bidirectional LSTM sees the whole window.

13
00:01:12.248 --> 00:01:22.938
Attention and Transformers scale to long sequences, and a sequence-to-sequence encoder–decoder handles multi-step output — but a simple naive or SARIMA baseline still has to be beaten.

14
00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:25.240
Forecasting several steps ahead has three flavors.

15
00:01:25.569 --> 00:01:28.577
Direct trains one model per horizon.

16
00:01:28.924 --> 00:01:33.143
Recursive feeds predictions back as inputs, so errors compound.

17
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:42.075
Seq2seq emits the whole horizon in one pass, and probabilistic models like DeepAR and the Temporal Fusion Transformer also give you calibrated uncertainty.

18
00:01:42.417 --> 00:01:43.100
Finally, anomaly detection.

19
00:01:43.442 --> 00:01:49.995
Train a model — often an LSTM autoencoder — to reconstruct normal windows.

20
00:01:50.307 --> 00:02:00.207
When reconstruction error spikes, you've found an anomaly; threshold it by a validation-set quantile, and let VAE variants give you calibrated scores.

21
00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:05.081
Let's build some forecasters — open the labs.
